For diplomas awarded in non-EU countries, a NARIC recognition is required.
Qualifications/diplomas awarded until 31/12/2020 in the United Kingdom are accepted without further recognition. For diplomas awarded after this date (from 01/01/2021), a NARIC recognition is required.
For native English speakers, your ability to communicate in another EU language will be tested during the selection process.
SELECTION CRITERIA
Essential:
Only applications meeting all essential selection criteria will be assessed.
Professional:
Candidates will be required to demonstrate the following qualifications:
a university degree (or equivalent academic qualification) in Law;
at least seven (7) years of relevant professional experience, acquired after the award of the minimum qualification required as a condition of eligibility;
substantial professional experience in providing legal advice on public and/or collaborative procurement, including complex or high-value procurement procedures and contractual arrangements;
proven experience in identifying and addressing legal and governance risks and in providing legal advice on complex institutional, contractual matters;
sound knowledge of the European Union data protection framework and proven professional experience in advising on data protection and compliance matters;
proven experience in coordinating the work and priorities of a legal team, including quality assurance and the provision of legal and procedural advice in a multidisciplinary environment;
good knowledge of EDA’s role, legal framework and main functions;
a very good command of written and spoken English.
Personal:
All staff must be able to fit into the Agency's way of working (see para. 2). Other attributes important for this post include:
ability to work independently and collaboratively in a complex and multidisciplinary environment;
ability to work effectively in a multinational environment;
reliability and ability to work under pressure with minimal supervision;
strong analytical and problem-solving skills, sound legal judgement, and the ability to identify proportionate, practical and defensible solutions;
excellent communication, interpersonal and negotiation skills, with the ability to explain complex legal issues clearly and influence stakeholders constructively;
good leadership skills and ability to coordinate and support colleagues effectively;
results-orientation and strong sense of responsibility;
flexibility and innovativeness;
genuine commitment to the Agency's objectives;
Desirable:
The following will be considered an advantage:
experience in litigation, pre-litigation or dispute management, for example before national courts, the Court of Justice of the European Union or in administrative complaint procedures;
knowledge of, or experience in addressing, the interaction between the Agency’s legal framework and other European Union legal or funding frameworks, including the European Defence Fund, the European Defence Industry Programme and other current or future initiatives relevant to defence and security;
familiarity with the defence international environment (especially with CSDP);
experience of contributing or leading organisational and process change.
