EU Digital Law and Legal Affairs Trainee
The Greens/EFA Group in the European Parliament
Brussels, Belgium
€ 1890 per month
About the traineeship
This traineeship offers first-hand experience of EU legislative work and the role of the European Parliament. You will deepen your understanding of key digital policy developments, including the European Innovation Act, the implementation of the EU digital rulebook, the EU Kids Act and forthcoming initiatives on digital transformation and copyright. You will also develop practical legal skills in research, legislative analysis and drafting, while discovering how political groups shape legislation, develop negotiation strategies and work across committees and EU institutions. Alongside digital policy, you will gain exposure to the broader legal and institutional responsibilities of the JURI Committee (Committee on Legal Affairs, including parliamentary immunities, actions before the Court of Justice of the European Union, the implementation of EU law and better law-making.
Main tasks and responsibilities
Working alongside your supervisor in the JURI Committee, you will contribute to activities such as:
Monitoring digital policy and legislative developments: Follow relevant news, policy developments and legislative initiatives in the fields of digital, tech and media regulation, including artificial intelligence, and copyright;
Legal research and legislative drafting: Support the analysis of legislative proposals and contribute to drafting amendments, particularly on files such as the European Innovation Act and other digital policy initiatives;
Political strategy and negotiations: Contribute ideas and legal analysis to the team's discussions on legislative compromises, amendments and negotiation strategies;
Briefings and parliamentary coordination: Help prepare and finalise written briefings ahead of committee meetings, ensuring that relevant information is organised and shared with colleagues in a timely manner;
JURI Working Group support: Assist with the organisation of internal JURI Working Group meetings, including calendar invitations, preparation of documents and coordination of meeting materials.
Depending on the legislative calendar, you may also have the opportunity to follow discussions and negotiations involving other parliamentary committees and EU institutions.
The traineeship is designed as a learning experience. Tasks and responsibilities will be adapted to the trainee's learning objectives, skills and level of experience, with appropriate guidance and supervision.
Who we are looking for
We are looking for someone who has some relevant knowledge or experience of EU law and who is motivated to learn about EU Digital Law, Legal Affairs, policy-making inside an EU institution with a political approach and contribute to the activities of the team.
We welcome applications from people at different stages of their education and career, including those looking to explore a new professional direction.
This role would suit someone who has:
Knowledge of EU law: A good understanding of EU law and the functioning of the European institutions, particularly the European Parliament and the EU legislative process;
Interest in European politics and digital policy: An interest in understanding how legal expertise is translated into political positions and legislative outcomes, and specific interest in areas such as digital, tech and media regulation, including artificial intelligence, copyright and innovation;
Legal research and analytical skills: The ability to understand complex legal and policy issues, identify relevant information and assess legislative proposals;
Writing and drafting skills: Very good written English and the ability to formulate clear, structured and precise arguments. Experience in legal or legislative drafting is an asset;
Critical and strategic thinking: The ability to analyse different perspectives, contribute ideas and consider the legal and political implications of proposed amendments and compromises in a dynamic and politically diverse environment;
Organisation and teamwork: The ability to manage information, prepare documents, respect deadlines and contribute constructively to collaborative work.
